I'm joined today by David litt who is a  former speechwriter for President Barack  Obama also author of the book thanks  Obama  my hope each Ange White House years  David it's so great to talk to you I was  very interested in the book let's start  with how you ended up in this position  of being a speechwriter particularly at  such a young age for President Obama I  was one of those college kids I  graduated college in 2008 and I didn't  plan on going into politics at all I was  trying to figure out what I wanted to do  I interned for the onion the the comedy  newspaper I applied to join the CIA and  was rejected and I'm January 3rd 2008 I  was watching the free cable  on a flight that little plane TV and the  seat back in front of me and it was CNN  was on and Barack Obama was delivering  his victory speech after the Iowa  caucuses and within a few minutes of  watching that speech I was one of those  young people whose lives was just  changed forever I mean by the time that  plane landed I was like okay whatever  this guy is doing I want to be part of  that does the CIA tell you why they've  rejected you when they reject you they  did in my case they they called me up  and they said let's just get kick things  off  have you used any illegal substances in  the last year and I thought about lying  to the CIA because I thought maybe  that's the and maybe this is a test but  actually I decided you know maybe I  shouldn't risk it so I told them the  truth I had smoked pot two months  earlier as my 21st birthday and they  instantly said you know okay well that's  it interview over  you know we remember they said we like  rule breakers but in this case we we  have to say we cut off the interview  unless you've you've been clear of  illegal substances for 12 months got it  so you enter and what are sort of the  initial speech writing assignments that  you do I started off at the White House  as a speechwriter to Valerie Jarrett who  was President Obama's senior adviser and  in a lot of ways it was a really lucky  job because she knew both the president  and the first lady for decades by the  time I met her and so I got an insight  into into the president that a lot of  people maybe wouldn't have gotten a  chance to because Valerie knew him so  well and then I also wrote for other  senior staff and for a long time my  niche with presidential speech writing  was basically what no one else wants to  do so if there was a video taping that  everybody else said you know we're  already busy this week and this doesn't  seem like a lot of fun and we're hoping  someone else can do it I was that  someone else for about a year or two and  gradually picked up more and more  responsibilities as time went on so  let's start with sort of those minor  speeches how much back and forth and  revising is there even for the really  minor speeches for the really minor  stuff there was not a ton of  back-and-forth with the president so for  example we would often take a big speech  that week and condensed it into that  week's weekly address I would sometimes  do the condensing but it's not like  President Obama was meeting with us to  say well I already delivered this speech  but this short version is it good also  you know he would trust that the process  would turn out something that was good  enough for that what we would have a lot  of is back and forth with the chief  speech writer who was jon favreau in my  in the first term my first few years  there and Cody Keenan in the second term  and he both of them knew the president's  voice really well and would be  responsible for going through it and  making sure that whatever I wrote or one  of our other speech writers wrote was  something that the president wouldn't  have to edit that intensely if it was  smaller and then the other thing we did  a lot of was fact-checking so even the  smallest videos were very intensely  fact-checked I imagine that sort of a  contrast with the current administration  I I imagine so yeah now as far as  writing for the president's style what  are some of the interesting particulars  that you could share of things you sort  of picked up in terms of the tone and  style that would be written in well the  biggest thing that I noticed about  writing for President Obama and it's one  thing I share in the book is he could  read a run-on sentence in a way that  most speakers cannot I mean if you're  watching this  and we're listening to this and you're  thinking I want to be a speech writer  one piece of advice I would normally  offer is write short sentences because  most speakers can't handle long  sentences they get lost in them  President Obama was such a good orator  and had such a good sense of audience  and timing that he could punctuate a  sentence himself so he could take a  run-on sentence and break it up  naturally and build to these crescendos  and often those were the most inspiring  moments in a speech so it was actually  something I almost had to unlearn and  relearn when I started writing for him  because in the past I had been working  very hard to keep sentences very very  short and now is this chance to kind of  unwind them and let him his sort of  natural extraordinary talent shine  through what in the world that you were  in of speech writing is considered sort  of like a major accomplishment or  success and what would be sort of like a  nice oh great moment well I think  there's all sorts of different  definitions of success I mean certainly  a big speech like the State of the Union  or a important speech something after a  national tragedy and I never held the  pen on any of those those were usually  the chief speechwriter or maybe the  deputy one one sort of rung down on the  speechwriting ladder but for me it was  you know the biggest features I probably  worked on were the Correspondents Dinner  speeches the jokes and the other thing I  write about a lot in the White House is  in a way I didn't fully appreciate when  I started there what success looks like  is managing to do your job in a way that  doesn't derail what everyone else is  trying to do in a big way because little  mistakes can have these huge  consequences I mean in the book I write  about time I accidentally caused an  international incident I write about a  time when Fox News picked up on some  small thing in a speech that I did and  it should have been innocuous but it  became a story for a day and so every  moment you're trying to not necessarily  make history not necessarily swing for  the fences but even in those less  historic moments you're just trying to  get it right let's talk about the  Correspondents Dinner how did you get  pulled into that was it because of your  background with the onion that sort of  made people aware like  maybe we want to talk to David litt  about this yeah I had what in DC passes  for a comedy background so I had my  onion internship I had done improv  comedy in college I started my speech  writing life as a writer at a firm  called West Wing writers which is a  private sector firm and one of the  speech writers there Jeff Nussbaum has a  niche where he writes a lot of jokes for  public figures so I had had some  experience writing jokes for politicians  too  and when I started at the White House  the speechwriter in charge of writing  jokes was John Lovett who's now a host  of POD save America and moved to LA and  he moved to LA in November 2011 so it  was really after he left that there is  the sense of like we need someone to be  our in-house token funny person and no  one else is there who's gonna fit the  bill so by default that kind of became  me so when you're writing jokes it would  seem to me that the standard for  evaluating how it went is very different  than when you're writing a serious  speech I mean how do you pay attention  to how that speech goes over I think  there were two important things for us  so one is when you're writing comedy  even for a politician even for a  president it has to be funny we were  always trying to do stuff that was  legitimately getting a laugh out of the  audience and it was easier for us than  for many other speech writers because  President Obama has such a good sense of  timing but it still takes a lot of work  and I go into all that work in the book  the other thing that was important for  me and for the the team of people  writing jokes was that in some small way  this was worth the president's time so  there's no question that going and doing  a correspondence that it was not the  most important thing President Obama was  gonna do that month or that week or  sometimes even that day but we still  tried to think about are we expressing  our point of view or we may be telling a  little truth about Washington are we  using self-deprecating jokes to make it  clear that while President Obama takes  his responsibilities seriously he  doesn't take himself too seriously so  even the things that seem totally  innocuous you think if the president is  gonna put his own time into making this  good then we want to make sure it in  some way helps him in his broader job so  no  what the process is behind the scenes or  at least what it was with President  Obama what do you make of or what do you  think is happening behind the scenes  with the writing of the speeches given  by the current president and and I don't  know if maybe the problems come when he  goes off of the written speech and maybe  if you have any experience and sort of  managing that didn't really seem to be a  concern with President Obama but what's  your analysis of what you see coming out  in terms of presidential speeches right  now so our goal I think it's fair to say  when the speechwriting team that I was a  part of our goal was always to make  President Obama sound like himself  because with the speeches we wrote I  would I wouldn't the highest compliment  I could imagine for a speech that I  wrote is is the kind of thing President  Obama would have written himself if he  had the time and I don't know that we  ever got quite there but the closer we  got the happier we were certainly the  happier I was um I think and in the book  I say that speech writers are more  personal trainers than puppet masters  they can make you present the best  version of yourself to the world they  can't put words in your mouth and turn  me into somebody or not and I think that  is the challenge for the current crop of  speech writers in the Trump White House  because President Trump is who he is and  I think we see him when he doesn't have  a script in front of him we see a  reflection of exactly who this person is  sometimes you get the sense that the  speech writers are hoping to hide who he  is by giving him remarks that you know  quote/unquote sound presidential but  it's not it's not gonna work in the long  term and so they're trying to do  something that speech writers have never  been able to do successfully and I think  I think this group of speech writers  isn't able to do successfully which is  pretend that somebody is a different  person than the one that we see  regularly on TV the other thing that's  interesting about this group of speech  writers is I think they have more  influence over the president then  particularly Stephen Miller the chief  speechwriter then other speech writers  in the past because Donald Trump is the  kind of person who listens to the last  person who was in the room he doesn't  have a thoughtful way of making  decisions and so what happens is  if Stephen Miller the chief speechwriter  is the last guy to edit the speech to  talk to the president he can manipulate  the president into saying something that  is closer to his kind of hardline white  nationalist view of the world then maybe  some of the president's other advisers  were there situations with President  Obama where for whatever reason he would  sort of start to depart from the written  speech and and riff and if so does that  worry you that maybe he doesn't like  what's been written or is it just sort  of hey this is President Obama he has  this ability to do this we don't worry  about it in the first term I think for  the most part President Obama was fairly  what's the right word he was you placed  a lot of stock in not ad-libbing unless  he felt he absolutely had to and the  reason for that is that anything that  you say in the first term of the  presidency can be taken out of context  and put in a political ad so it's not  just a question of what you're trying to  get across but whether someone can  misinterpret that and that's hard to  judge and figure out on the fly in the  second term I do think President Obama  went off-script a little more I know  with the jokes there were one or two  times he improvised the whole run of  jokes and you to his credit would never  know just watching it which ones were  scripted and which ones weren't so to  your question honestly there weren't a  lot of times when he would go off script  and I'm worried that it meant that he  didn't like the remarks at all because  they weren't usually totally changing  everything generally speaking I was kind  of curious to see what he would say and  also aware that odds were pretty good  that that was gonna be the part of the  speech where you know everybody my  friends and family would say like that  was amazing did you write that part and  I'd say well you know I wrote the speech  like yeah try to try to dodge the  question a little bit so we were very  lucky not many speech writers get to  work for someone who when they start to  go off script you think oh this is gonna  be good  I think a lot of speech writers have the  opposite feeling yeah particularly right  now I would say we've been speaking with  David litt former speechwriter for  President Barack Obama the book which is  fascinating is thanks Obama my hope each  ange White House years David thanks so  much for talking to us and best of luck  with the book thank you so much it's  great to be here

